# Simulation output: several GB of .jld2 per sweep, and fully regenerable from
# the scripts + case tags. Keep it out of the repo so `git add -A` stays safe.
#
# `outputs/**` rather than `outputs/`, paired with the `!outputs/**/` below:
# git does not descend into an EXCLUDED DIRECTORY, so with a plain `outputs/`
# no rule further down can ever re-include a file inside it. Excluding the
# contents while re-including the directories themselves lets git look inside,
# which is what makes the animation exception at the bottom work.
outputs/**
!outputs/**/

# Animations are large, but they are a deliverable and the only way they reach a
# workstation is through the repo (rsync from the cluster needs an inbound route
# that is not there). They used to need `git add -f` case by case; the negation
# below means `git add -A` picks up new ones on its own.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: the last matching rule wins, so this negation must stay AFTER
# the blanket *.mp4. Everything else under outputs/ — .jld2 fields, slices,
# checkpoints — stays ignored.
*.mp4
!outputs/**/animation_*.mp4

# Machine-specific CUDA runtime selection (CUDA.set_runtime_version!). On the
# cluster this pins CUDA.jl to the module-provided toolkit instead of pulling a
# ~1 GB artifact; on a workstation the artifact is the right choice and the same
# file would break it. It must stay local to whichever machine wrote it.
LocalPreferences.toml
